        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n### Question 8: Cookie consent — what are we actually consenting to?\n\nGDPR requires consent before setting non-essential cookies/tracking. Let's inventory what the app actually stores:\n\n| Storage | What | Essential? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `localStorage` | JWT token | **Essential** — required for auth to function |\n| `sessionStorage` | Impersonation original JWT | **Essential** — admin impersonation mechanism |\n| `localStorage` | Dark mode preference (planned) | **Technically non-essential, but functionally exempt** — \"preference cookies\" that don't track users |\n| `localStorage` | Language preference (planned, from i18next) | Same as above — preference, no tracking |\n| Future: analytics? | N/A — don't think you're adding Google Analytics | — |\n\nHere's the thing: with **zero tracking cookies**, the GDPR \"cookie wall\" can be very lightweight — essentially a notice that says \"We use localStorage to keep you logged in and remember your preferences. No tracking.\" With just an \"OK\" button, not accept/reject categories.\n\nTwo approaches:\n\n| Approach | UX | Effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A: Bare-minimum notice** | Small banner at bottom: \"This demo uses browser storage for authentication and preferences. No tracking. [OK]\" — one click, disappears. | Minimal — a single component, ~30 lines. |\n| **B: Full GDPR banner** | \"Accept All / Essential Only / Customize\" with category toggles. | Overkill for a demo with zero tracking. |\n\nRecommendation: **A** — honest, minimal, matches the sarcastic/self-aware tone of the site. A full GDPR banner with \"customize\" when there's nothing to customize feels dishonest.\n\nConfirm A, or want the full treatment?",
          "time": {
