          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "OK so:\n\n1. No existing a11y tools in the frontend project\n2. `@axe-core/cli` (the free open-source CLI) **works** — version 9.1.1 is available\n3. `pa11y` — not available (but can be installed)\n4. Lighthouse — not installed globally, but it's built into Chrome DevTools (free!)\n\nThe best free approach:\n- **Lighthouse in Chrome DevTools** — zero install, free, open source. Open the app, F12, Lighthouse tab, uncheck everything except Accessibility, run audit per page. This gives a score + violation list.\n- **`@axe-core/cli`** (free CLI) — can run `npx @axe-core/cli http://localhost:5173/login` etc. But this requires the page to be fully server-rendered or it just captures the initial HTML, not the React-rendered DOM. So it's less useful for SPAs unless we use something like `axe-core` with Puppeteer/Playwright.\n- **Add `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y`** — free static analysis in the editor/CI. Catches missing `alt`, `aria-*` issues, etc. at code time.\n\nLet me be practical. Lighthouse is the best free tool for this since it's built into Chrome. Let me suggest:\n\n1. Lighthouse (built-in, free) — the primary tool\n2. `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y` — add to the project for ongoing static analysis (free, code-level)\n\nThe axe-core CLI with npx works but requires full page renders (not great for SPA). Lighthouse is better because it runs in the browser context.\n\nLet me give the user a practical guide focused on Lighthouse + the ESLint plugin.",
